":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grand_Prismatic_Spring","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","attribution":"Wikipedia"}},{"id":"way/24064939","name":"Grebe Lake","kind":"lake","lon":-110.55847,"lat":44.75174,"ele_m":null,"wikidata":"Q5601517","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grebe_Lake"},{"id":"way/184500195","name":"Heart Lake","kind":"lake","lon":-110.47666,"lat":44.26754,"ele_m":null,"wikidata":"Q1592389","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heart_Lake_(Wyoming)"},{"id":"way/24406788","name":"Lewis Lake","kind":"lake","lon":-110.62846,"lat":44.29994,"ele_m":null,"wikidata":"Q1616647","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lewis_Lake_(Wyoming)"},{"id":"way/23685612","name":"Shoshone Lake","kind":"lake","lon":-110.72798,"lat":44.37435,"ele_m":null,"wikidata":"Q1524895","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shoshone_Lake"},{"id":"way/39263645","name":"Turbid Lake","kind":"lake","lon":-110.25983,"lat":44.549,"ele_m":null,"wikidata":"Q21197299","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Turbid_Lake"},{"id":"way/39263622","name":"Wrangler Lake","kind":"lake","lon":-110.43584,"lat":44.68326,"ele_m":null,"wikidata":"Q7332047","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wrangler_Lake"},{"id":"relation/207316","name":"Yellowstone Lake","kind":"lake","lon":-110.39122,"lat":44.42035,"ele_m":2357.0,"wikidata":"Q923693","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yellowstone_Lake","tour":3,"summary":{"extract":"Yellowstone Lake is the largest body of water in Wyoming and the largest in Yellowstone National Park. The lake is 7,732 feet (2,357 m) above sea level and covers 136 square miles (350 km2) with 110 miles (180 km) of shoreline. While the average depth of the lake is 139 ft (42 m), its greatest depth is at least 394 ft (120 m). Yellowstone Lake is the largest freshwater lake above 7,000 ft (2,100 m) in North America.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yellowstone_Lake","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","attribution":"Wikipedia"}},{"id":"way/39263937","name":"Alder Lake","kind":"lake","lon":-110.31181,"lat":44.33671,"ele_m":2363.0,"wikidata":"Q49298801","url":null},{"id":"way/39263930","name":"Beach Lake","kind":"lake","lon":-110.57056,"lat":44.5411,"ele_m":2483.0,"wikidata":"Q49299169","url":null},{"id":"node/7813506978","name":"Mammoth Hot Springs Terraces","kind":"attraction","lon":-110.70564,"lat":44.97062,"ele_m":null,"wikidata":"Q1013311","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mammoth_Hot_Springs","tour":9,"summary":{"extract":"Mammoth Hot Springs is a large complex of hot springs on a hill of travertine in Yellowstone National Park adjacent to Fort Yellowstone and the Mammoth Hot Springs Historic District. It was created over thousands of years as hot water from the spring cooled and deposited calcium carbonate. Because of the huge amount of geothermal vents, travertine flourishes. Although these springs lie outside the caldera boundary, their energy has been attributed to the same magmatic system that fuels other Yellowstone geothermal areas.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mammoth_Hot_Springs","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","attribution":"Wikipedia"}},{"id":"node/4332318698","name":"West Thumb Geyser Basin","kind":"attraction","lon":-110.57197,"lat":44.41701,"ele_m":null,"wikidata":"Q15134786","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/West_Thumb_Geyser_Basin","tour":2,"summary":{"extract":"The geothermal areas of Yellowstone include several geyser basins in Yellowstone National Park as well as other geothermal features such as hot springs, mud pots, and fumaroles. The number of thermal features in Yellowstone is estimated at 10,000. A study that was completed in 2011 found that a total of 1,283 geysers have erupted in Yellowstone, 465 of which are active during an average year. These are distributed among nine geyser basins, with a few geysers found in smaller thermal areas throughout the Park. The number of geysers in each geyser basin are as follows: Upper Geyser Basin (410), Midway Geyser Basin (59), Lower Geyser Basin (283), Norris Geyser Basin (193), West Thumb Geyser Basin (84), Gibbon Geyser Basin (24), Lone Star Geyser Basin (21), Shoshone Geyser Basin (107), Heart Lake Geyser Basin (69), other areas (33). Although famous large geysers like Old Faithful are part of the total, most of Yellowstone's geysers are small, erupting to only a foot or two. The hydrothermal system that supplies the geysers with hot water sits within an ancient active caldera. Many of the thermal features in Yellowstone build up sinter, geyserite, or travertine deposits around and within them.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geothermal_areas_of_Yellowstone","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","attribution":"Wikipedia"}},{"id":"node/4332318504","name":"Hayden Valley","kind":"place","lon":-110.45622,"lat":44.64362,"ele_m":null,"wikidata":"Q463354","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hayden_Valley","tour":4,"summary":{"extract":"Hayden Valley is a large, sub-alpine valley within Yellowstone National Park straddling the Yellowstone River between Yellowstone Falls and Yellowstone Lake. The valley floor along the river is an ancient lake bed from a time when Yellowstone Lake was much larger.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hayden_Valley","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","attribution":"Wikipedia"}},{"id":"config/5","name":"Lower Falls","kind":"place","lon":-110.4967,"lat":44.718,"ele_m":null,"wikidata":null,"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lower_Falls_of_the_Yellowstone_River","tour":5,"summary":null},{"id":"config/8","name":"Lamar Valley","kind":"place","lon":-110.26,"lat":44.905,"ele_m":null,"wikidata":null,"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lamar_Valley","tour":8,"summary":{"extract":"The Lamar River is a tributary of the Yellowstone River, approximately 40 miles (64 km) long, in northwestern Wyoming in the United States. The river is located entirely within Yellowstone National Park.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lamar_River","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","attribution":"Wikipedia"}},{"id":"config/10","name":"Norris Geyser Basin","kind":"place","lon":-110.703,"lat":44.726,"ele_m":null,"wikidata":null,"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Norris_Geyser_Basin","tour":10,"summary":{"extract":"The geothermal areas of Yellowstone include several geyser basins in Yellowstone National Park as well as other geothermal features such as hot springs, mud pots, and fumaroles. The number of thermal features in Yellowstone is estimated at 10,000. A study that was completed in 2011 found that a total of 1,283 geysers have erupted in Yellowstone, 465 of which are active during an average year. These are distributed among nine geyser basins, with a few geysers found in smaller thermal areas throughout the Park. The number of geysers in each geyser basin are as follows: Upper Geyser Basin (410), Midway Geyser Basin (59), Lower Geyser Basin (283), Norris Geyser Basin (193), West Thumb Geyser Basin (84), Gibbon Geyser Basin (24), Lone Star Geyser Basin (21), Shoshone Geyser Basin (107), Heart Lake Geyser Basin (69), other areas (33). Although famous large geysers like Old Faithful are part of the total, most of Yellowstone's geysers are small, erupting to only a foot or two. The hydrothermal system that supplies the geysers with hot water sits within an ancient active caldera. Many of the thermal features in Yellowstone build up sinter, geyserite, or travertine deposits around and within them.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geothermal_areas_of_Yellowstone","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","attribution":"Wikipedia"}}],"tour":["node/356502828","way/27750226","node/4332318698","relation/207316","node/4332318504","config/5","node/261131064","node/2488864145","config/8","node/7813506978","config/10"],"missing_stops":[]}